'''Corazon Magalona-Treñas''' (born August 21, 1952) is a Dagiti politician and businesswoman who has served as the [[Vice President of Dagit]] since June 30, 2023. She previously served as the [[Mayor of Akyatan]] from 2010 to 2023, a position she won in a landslide election following the [[Dagiti Revolution of 2009]] that ousted her husband, President [[Ramon Magalona]], from power. She is the mother of current President [[Enrique Magalona]], making her the first person in Dagiti history to serve as vice president to her own child.

Treñas is widely credited with preserving the Magalona political brand during her husband's fourteen-year exile in [[Volisania]]. While Ramon Magalona fled the country to avoid prosecution for the [[Bulkan Pondo Scandal]], Treñas remained in Dagit, won the mayorship of the nation's capital, and systematically rebuilt the family's political network. Her survival and subsequent political ascent—transforming from the disgraced first lady to the elected mayor of the nation's largest city, then to the vice presidency—represents one of the most remarkable political rehabilitations in modern Dagiti history.

== Early life and education ==
Corazon Treñas was born on August 21, 1952, in the [[Barrio Treñas]] district of [[Akyatan]], the eldest daughter of Salvador Treñas and Maria Elena Treñas (née Yanson). The Treñas family was part of the "new merchant oligarchy" that emerged in post-war Dagit, having built their fortune in shipping and warehousing through connections with the [[Cinc Parets–Macaio Galleon Trade|galleon trade]] networks.

Her mother, Maria Elena, was a member of the powerful Yanson family, which controlled tourism and transportation interests throughout the archipelago. This maternal connection would prove crucial to the family's political survival decades later, when the Yanson resources funded the rehabilitation of the Magalona brand.

Treñas attended the [[Colegio de Akyatan]], the elite Volisanian-language institution serving the children of the merchant oligarchy. She excelled academically and was noted for her fluency in both Dagiti and Volisanian, a skill that would serve her throughout her political career. In 1970, she enrolled at the [[University of Akyatan]], where she studied business administration with a concentration in maritime commerce, graduating in 1974.

== Marriage to Ramon Magalona ==
In 1975, Treñas married [[Ramon Magalona]], then a rising movie star and recording artist. The marriage united two powerful families: the Treñas-Yanson merchant interests and the Magalona entertainment dynasty. Ramon Magalona's father, Fernando Magalona Sr., had been a famous actor and recording artist, while his mother claimed descent from [[Mateu Malvar]], the revolutionary leader and first president of the Republic of Dagit.

The couple had three children: [[Enrique Magalona|Enrique]] (born 1977), Maria Elena (born 1979), and Ramon Jr. (born 1982). Throughout the early years of their marriage, Treñas focused on managing the family's business interests while her husband pursued his entertainment career. She was frequently described in the press as the "power behind the throne," handling financial matters and maintaining relationships with the merchant families who would later become the backbone of Magalona's political support.

=== Entry into politics ===
When Ramon Magalona transitioned from entertainment to politics in the 1990s, Treñas played a crucial behind-the-scenes role. She managed campaign finances, cultivated relationships with the Yanson and Treñas family networks, and coordinated the celebrity endorsements that became the hallmark of Magalona campaigns. Her political acumen was noted by observers who recognized that her business background and family connections made her an effective political operator.

During her husband's presidency (1998–2009), Treñas maintained a relatively low public profile compared to previous first ladies. She focused on charitable initiatives and avoided direct involvement in governance, a decision that would later prove advantageous when the administration became engulfed in scandal. However, she was known to have significant influence over presidential appointments, particularly those involving the Yanson family's business interests.

== The Bulkan Pondo Scandal and family crisis ==
{{main|Bulkan Pondo Scandal|Dagiti Revolution of 2009}}
The [[Bulkan Pondo Scandal]], which broke in 2006, revealed systematic looting of public funds during Ramon Magalona's presidency. An estimated ₱2.3 billion in tobacco tax revenues intended for local government development projects had been diverted through inflated contracts, ghost projects, and direct kickbacks to administration officials.

While Treñas was never personally implicated in the corruption, the scandal destroyed her husband's presidency. The [[Katipunan sa Katarungan]] (Society for Justice) organized mass demonstrations in [[Plaza sang Akyatan]], echoing the revolutionary tradition of 1850. The [[Dagiti Revolution of 2009]] culminated on October 15, 2009, when President Magalona resigned and fled into exile in [[Volisania]].

Crucially, Treñas chose not to accompany her husband into exile. In a decision that would define her political future, she remained in Dagit with her children and faced the revolutionary government. This choice—characterized by supporters as courage and by critics as calculated political survival—allowed her to maintain her family's connections to the Treñas and Yanson networks while her husband bore the brunt of public outrage.

== Mayor of Akyatan (2010–2023) ==

=== 2010 election ===
In November 2009, just weeks after her husband's resignation, Treñas announced her candidacy for mayor of [[Akyatan]] in the 2010 local elections. The announcement stunned political observers, who expected the Magalona family to retreat permanently from public life.

Treñas campaigned on a platform of "healing and reconstruction," carefully distancing herself from her husband's administration while emphasizing her own family's contributions to the city. She highlighted her Treñas ancestry—the family's centuries-old presence in the historic [[Barrio Treñas]] district—and her maternal connections to the Yanson family's business empire. The campaign raised an unprecedented ₱800 million, primarily from Yanson interests, enabling Treñas to outspend her opponents by a margin of four to one.

On May 10, 2010, Treñas was elected mayor of Akyatan with 62% of the vote, defeating the candidate of the revolutionary coalition that had ousted her husband. Her victory was widely interpreted as a rejection of the post-revolutionary government's failure to deliver economic improvement and as evidence of the Magalona family's enduring political appeal.

=== Mayoral administration ===
Treñas served as mayor of Akyatan for three consecutive terms (2010–2023), using the position to systematically rebuild the Magalona political network. Her administration focused on:

* Infrastructure development: Expanding the [[Akyatan Metropolitan Expressway]] and modernizing the port facilities that served the city's commercial interests
* Heritage preservation: Restoring historic sites within the [[Cinc Parets]] walls, emphasizing the city's role in Dagiti revolutionary history
* Social welfare programs: Establishing the ''Kabalikat sa Kaunlaran'' program, which provided direct assistance to low-income families in exchange for community service

Critics noted that Treñas's infrastructure projects frequently benefited Yanson family construction interests, while her social welfare programs created a patronage network that would later support her son's presidential campaign. Nevertheless, her mayoralty was generally regarded as competent and corruption-free, a sharp contrast to her husband's scandal-plagued presidency.

The most significant achievement of Treñas's mayoral administration was the rehabilitation of the Magalona brand. Through carefully managed public appearances, strategic philanthropy, and the cultivation of local media, Treñas gradually reshaped public perception of her family. By 2020, polls showed that a majority of Akyatan residents viewed the Magalona family favorably—a remarkable turnaround from the depths of 2009.

=== Role in Enrique Magalona's rise ===
Throughout her mayoralty, Treñas worked behind the scenes to position her son [[Enrique Magalona]] for a presidential run. She maintained relationships with the Yanson family and other merchant interests, ensuring that funding would be available when the time came. She also cultivated connections with media figures and social media influencers who would later amplify her son's message.

When Enrique Magalona launched his television career in 2016, Treñas provided strategic guidance and access to the family's political network. Her experience as mayor informed the campaign's emphasis on "unity and reconciliation," which allowed Enrique to present himself as a unifying figure rather than the heir to a disgraced dynasty.

== Vice presidency (2023–present) ==

=== 2023 election ===
{{main|2023 Dagiti presidential election}}
In January 2023, Treñas announced that she would run for vice president alongside her son's presidential campaign. The announcement was unprecedented in Dagiti history—a mother-and-son ticket for the nation's two highest offices.

The decision drew immediate criticism from opponents who characterized it as a naked power grab by the Magalona family. Critics noted that the arrangement would effectively concentrate executive power within a single family, raising concerns about democratic accountability. Treñas defended the ticket by citing her experience as mayor and her commitment to supporting her son's agenda.

The Magalona campaign's overwhelming financial advantage—an estimated ₱5 billion war chest from Yanson and other merchant interests—overwhelmed the fragmented opposition. On May 18, 2023, the Magalona-Treñas ticket won with 38.2% of the presidential vote and 41.5% of the vice presidential vote, making Corazon Treñas the first person in Dagiti history to be elected vice president alongside her own child.

=== Vice-presidential role ===
As vice president, Treñas has taken on a prominent role in the administration's economic initiatives. She serves as chair of the [[Kongreso sang Dagit|National Economic Advisory Council]] and has led trade delegations to [[Okaiken]], [[Yanbaru]], and [[Balisca]]. Her business background and family connections have proven valuable in negotiations with merchant interests and foreign investors.

Treñas has also maintained an active role in Akyatan politics, where her allies continue to dominate the city council. Critics have accused her of using the vice presidency to advance her family's local interests, particularly in heritage tourism and port development projects that benefit Yanson family concessions.

== Personal life ==
Corazon Treñas's relationship with her former husband, [[Ramon Magalona]], remains a subject of public speculation. The two have not divorced, but Ramon Magalona remains in exile in Volisania and did not return to Dagit for his son's inauguration. Treñas has declined to comment on the status of their marriage, saying only that she remains "focused on serving the people of Dagit."

She maintains residences in the [[Barrio Treñas]] district of Akyatan and in the vice-presidential residence in the capital. Her daughter Maria Elena manages the family's business interests, while her younger son Ramon Jr. has pursued a career in entertainment.

Treñas is an adherent of the syncretic Anitist-Volisanian religious tradition prevalent in Dagit. She has been noted for her regular attendance at religious festivals and her patronage of religious institutions, particularly those associated with the [[babaylan]] priestess tradition.

== Controversies ==
Treñas has faced criticism for several aspects of her political career:

* Nepotism concerns: The mother-and-son ticket raised questions about family concentration of power unprecedented in Dagiti history
* Yanson connections: Critics have alleged that her administration consistently favored Yanson family business interests in infrastructure and tourism contracts
* Historical revisionism: Treñas has been accused of supporting efforts to minimize the [[Bulkan Pondo Scandal]] in educational materials, reframing her husband's ouster as a political persecution rather than a response to corruption

Despite these controversies, Treñas has maintained relatively high approval ratings throughout her vice presidency, benefiting from her son's popularity and her reputation for competent governance as mayor.

